<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android's backup service is allowed to carry off this device, on
  Android 11 and below. The Android 12+ rules live in data_extraction_rules.xml
  and say exactly the same thing in the newer grammar; both have to exist or
  the older devices fall back to "back up everything", which is the opposite of
  what is wanted here.

  ── Why backup is on at all ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  It was off. It is on because Zero-Tap Sign-In needs it: the restore key the
  Restore Credentials API mints is carried to a member's next handset by the
  backup transport, and an app excluded from backup is an app the transport
  carries nothing for. Google Play requires that restore from April 2027.

  ── Why almost nothing is listed ──────────────────────────────────────────
  An <include> list is a whitelist: naming one file excludes every other file,
  database and preference the app has. That is deliberate, and the one entry
  below is the app's own preferences — appearance, reading language, data-saver
  choices, recent searches, Kawuri history. Restoring those is the point of
  pairing backup with the restore key: the new phone opens in dark mode, in the
  right language, rather than as a stranger.

  Everything else stays behind, and each omission is a decision:

    * FlutterSecureStorage.xml — encrypted with keys held in this device's
      hardware keystore. Those keys are not backed up and cannot be, so the
      restored file would be ciphertext nobody can read. Copying it to another
      handset achieves nothing and moves a secret for no reason.
    * The Firebase Auth session store — a live session is bound to the install
      that obtained it. The whole design here is that the *new* device proves
      itself with a restore key and is issued its own session.
    * databases/ — the offline library index. It names downloaded media files
      by path, and those files are not restored, so a copied index describes a
      library that is not there.
    * files/ and external storage — downloaded audio and video. Large, and
      re-downloadable on demand.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="sharedpref" path="FlutterSharedPreferences.xml"/>
</full-backup-content>
